    "q-181950": {
      "dom_id": "step-q181950",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=N\\times {0,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times {0,1}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is either $0$ or $1.$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in {0,1}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any natural number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $0.$ \n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(x,0)\\,:\\,x\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/174a8dde6f409fe9f4f0aa3ea204c3b438a35b1bae607ebe2b101139fb807b99.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any natural number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$ \n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/a075d3109fdbd861c1e3e3103095f88691d10bcebb5708989640ca9e0a1c8931.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times {0,1}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/9c3083a4394093f53ff23f74f8b37e29d20da7cb693b2755fedfbe085b0ecf11.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
